  "content": {
    "id": "v2-a6564-p25-practice-slot-8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Compare the domain of $f(g(x))$ and $g(f(x))$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "v2-a6564-p25-practice-slot-8-slot-8-q-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}$ and $g(x)=x^2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(g(x))=\\sqrt{x^2+1}$ has domain $x\\in\\mathbb R$, while $g(f(x))=x+1$ has domain $x\\ge-1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(g(x))&=\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\end{align*}$$\nSince $x^2+1>0$ for all $x$, the domain is $\\mathbb R$.\n$$\\begin{align*}g(f(x))&=(\\sqrt{x+1})^2\\\\g(f(x))&=x+1\\end{align*}$$\nThe inner function $f(x)$ requires $x+1\\ge0$, so the domain is $x\\ge-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 50
      },
      {
        "id": "v2-a6564-p25-practice-slot-8-slot-8-q-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $g(x)=x^2-4$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(g(x))=\\dfrac{1}{x^2-4}$ has domain $x\\in\\mathbb R$, $x\\ne\\pm2$, while $g(f(x))=\\dfrac{1}{x^2}-4$ has domain $x\\in\\mathbb R$, $x\\ne0$.",
          "worked": "For $f(g(x))$, the denominator must be nonzero:\n$$\\begin{align*}f(g(x))&=\\frac{1}{x^2-4}\\\\x^2-4&\\ne0\\\\x&\\ne\\pm2\\end{align*}$$\nFor $g(f(x))$:\n$$\\begin{align*}g(f(x))&=\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^2-4\\\\g(f(x))&=\\frac{1}{x^2}-4\\end{align*}$$\nThe inner function $f(x)$ requires $x\\ne0$, so the domain is $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0\\}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 50
      }
    ]
  },
